<sect1 id="hetero_1autotoc_md28">
<title>Single-Agent Heterogeneity</title><para>The above example is one way to simulate heterogeneous dynamics, but not the only way. One could just as easily combine the state vectors of all the vertices into a single, contiguous vector, and define the heterogeneous dynamics in the dynamics function. The code is identical to <ref refid="highorder" kindref="compound">Simulating Higher-Order Dynamics</ref>, and indeed one could consider a coupled system of ODEs as having distinct dynamics equations, because the right-hand side of the equation differs for each variable. As an example, consider a Lotka-Volterra type interaction between a predator <formula id="76">$N_{pred}$</formula> and prey <formula id="77">$N_{prey}$</formula> population, given by</para>
<para><formula id="78">\[    \frac{{\rm d}N_{prey}}{{\rm d}t} = \alpha N_{prey} - \beta N_{prey}N_{pred},
\]</formula>   <formula id="79">\[    \frac{{\rm d}N_{pred}}{{\rm d}t} = \delta N_{prey}N_{pred} - \gamma N_{pred}.
\]</formula></para>
<para>Instead of defining separate vertices for the populations, we shall use a single vertex whose state vector contains both populations. In our dynamics function, we then simply write either equation to its corresponding index in the output vector.</para>
<para><programlisting filename=".c"><codeline><highlight class="keywordtype">int</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>lv(<ref refid="ndxlcore_8h_1ad03a49b11a22416ba63273c4d7deb38b" kindref="member">NDXLVertex</ref>*<sp/>pops,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal">*<sp/>dN,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="keyword">const</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal">*<sp/>N,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>t,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">void</highlight><highlight class="normal">*<sp/>p)</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>alpha<sp/>=<sp/>((</highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal">*)p)[0];</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>beta<sp/>=<sp/>((</highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal">*)p)[1];</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>delta<sp/>=<sp/>((</highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">double</highlight><highlight class="normal">*)p)[2];</highlight></codeline>
